D={
"Opium Wars":"British merchants smuggled opium grown in Bengal into China to pay for tea, creating millions of addicts. When Commissioner Lin Zexu destroyed 1,400 tonnes of opium at Canton in 1839, Britain sent gunboats; the Treaty of Nanking (1842) ceded Hong Kong, opened five ports and imposed an indemnity. A Second Opium War (1856–60) with France ended with the looting and burning of the Old Summer Palace and legalised opium. China calls the era that followed the 'century of humiliation'.",
"Abolition of slavery in the British Empire":"Campaigners such as Olaudah Equiano, Thomas Clarkson and William Wilberforce, along with major slave revolts in Jamaica (Baptist War, 1831–32), pushed Parliament to end the slave trade (1807) and then slavery itself. The Slavery Abolition Act of 1833 freed about 800,000 people from 1834, though most had to serve 'apprenticeships' until 1838. Slave owners received £20 million in compensation — the enslaved received nothing. Plantation owners then turned to indentured labourers from India, sending over a million to the Caribbean, Mauritius, Fiji and Africa.",
"American Civil War":"When Abraham Lincoln was elected in 1860 on a platform opposing slavery's expansion, eleven southern states seceded as the Confederacy. War began at Fort Sumter in 1861; key battles included Antietam, Gettysburg (1863) and Sherman's march through Georgia. About 620,000–750,000 soldiers died. Lincoln's Emancipation Proclamation (1863) and the 13th Amendment (1865) abolished slavery; Lincoln was assassinated days after the Confederacy surrendered at Appomattox.",
"Scramble for Africa":"In 1870 Europeans controlled about 10% of Africa; by 1914 only Ethiopia and Liberia remained independent. Quinine, steamships and the Maxim gun gave Europeans an edge. Borders drawn at the Berlin Conference (1884–85) ignored ethnic and linguistic realities. Colonial rule brought forced labour, the Congo atrocities, the Herero and Nama genocide in German South-West Africa, and economies built around exporting raw materials.",
"Great Famine of 1876–78":"A failed monsoon linked to a strong El Niño hit Madras, Mysore, Hyderabad and Bombay presidencies. Viceroy Lytton, following free-market principles, refused to control prices while grain was exported to Britain, and relief camps gave rations (the 'Temple wage') below those in Buchenwald, as historian Mike Davis notes. Some 5.5–10 million people died. Outrage over this famine helped give birth to Indian economic nationalism and the Congress.",
"Founding of the Indian National Congress":"Educated Indians frustrated by limited representation met in Bombay in December 1885, with 72 delegates under W. C. Bonnerjee, helped by retired civil servant A. O. Hume. The Moderates (Naoroji, Gokhale) petitioned for reforms; Extremists (Tilak, Lajpat Rai, Bipin Chandra Pal) demanded swaraj. Under Gandhi from 1920 it became a mass movement and later India's ruling party.",
"Partition of Bengal":"In 1905 Viceroy Curzon divided Bengal into a Muslim-majority east and Hindu-majority west, officially for administration but widely seen as divide and rule. Bengalis protested with the Swadeshi movement — boycotting British cloth, burning foreign goods, singing Tagore's songs — and revolutionary groups formed. The partition was annulled in 1911, and the capital moved from Calcutta to Delhi.",
"World War I":"Rivalries, an arms race and alliances turned the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo (28 June 1914) into a world war. Trench warfare on the Western Front, poison gas, tanks, machine guns and aircraft killed about 9–10 million soldiers and millions of civilians. About 1.3 million Indian soldiers served in France, Mesopotamia, Gallipoli and East Africa, and some 74,000 died. The war destroyed the German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian and Ottoman empires and set the stage for World War II.",
"Russian Revolution":"Food shortages and military defeats led to strikes in Petrograd in February 1917; Tsar Nicholas II abdicated. A Provisional Government continued the war, and in October the Bolsheviks under Lenin seized power with the slogan 'Peace, land, bread'. A civil war (1918–21) killed millions and created the Soviet Union in 1922.",
"Jallianwala Bagh massacre":"After protests against the Rowlatt Act, which allowed imprisonment without trial, crowds gathered at Jallianwala Bagh in Amritsar on Baisakhi, 13 April 1919. Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer blocked the narrow exit and ordered troops to fire for about ten minutes; official figures said 379 died, Indian estimates over 1,000. Tagore renounced his knighthood, and Udham Singh later shot Michael O'Dwyer, Punjab's former governor, in London (1940).",
"Non-Cooperation Movement":"Launched by Gandhi in 1920 with the Khilafat movement, it asked Indians to give up titles, boycott government schools, courts, legislatures and foreign cloth, and spin khadi. Millions joined, uniting Hindus and Muslims. After a mob killed 22 policemen at Chauri Chaura in February 1922, Gandhi called it off, insisting on non-violence.",
"Great Depression":"After the Wall Street crash of October 1929, banks failed, trade collapsed and world unemployment soared — in the US to 25%. Farm prices fell, hurting India's peasants. Desperation helped Hitler win power in 1933, while Roosevelt's New Deal expanded government's role.",
"Salt March":"On 12 March 1930 Gandhi and 78 volunteers left Sabarmati Ashram and walked 385 km to Dandi, where on 6 April he picked up salt, breaking the British salt monopoly. Civil disobedience spread; about 60,000 were arrested. The march drew world attention.",
"World War II":"Germany's invasion of Poland on 1 September 1939 started the war in Europe; Japan's attack on Pearl Harbor (1941) widened it. The Axis was defeated at Stalingrad, El Alamein, Midway, and on D-Day (1944). About 70–85 million people died, including the 6 million Jews of the Holocaust and about 27 million Soviet citizens. India sent 2.5 million volunteers; the war hastened decolonisation and created the UN and Cold War.",
"The Holocaust":"The Nazis' antisemitic laws from 1933 escalated into ghettos, mass shootings by Einsatzgruppen, and death camps such as Auschwitz, Treblinka and Sobibor. About 6 million Jews were murdered, along with Roma, disabled people, Soviet POWs and others. The Nuremberg Trials and the Genocide Convention (1948) followed.",
"Quit India Movement":"On 8 August 1942 Congress demanded an immediate British withdrawal, Gandhi calling 'Do or die'. Leaders were arrested next morning, and spontaneous protests, strikes and sabotage spread; about 100,000 were arrested and hundreds killed. Parallel governments formed in Satara, Tamluk and Ballia.",
"Bengal famine of 1943":"The loss of Burmese rice after Japan's conquest, a cyclone, wartime inflation, hoarding, and British 'denial' policy, plus Churchill's war cabinet refusing food imports, caused a famine that killed about 3 million. Amartya Sen's work showed it was a failure of entitlement, not of food supply.",
"Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki":"On 6 August 1945 the US dropped 'Little Boy' on Hiroshima and on 9 August 'Fat Man' on Nagasaki. About 110,000–210,000 died by the end of 1945. Japan surrendered on 15 August. Survivors (hibakusha) suffered radiation sickness.",
"Indian Independence and Partition":"Britain, exhausted, agreed to leave; Mountbatten brought the date forward to August 1947. The Radcliffe Line divided Punjab and Bengal; about 15 million people crossed borders and up to 1–2 million were killed. Kashmir's accession led to war.",
"Indian Constitution adopted":"The Constituent Assembly debated for nearly three years; the drafting committee was chaired by Ambedkar. Adopted on 26 November 1949 and in force on 26 January 1950, it guaranteed fundamental rights, universal adult franchise and abolished untouchability.",
"Chinese Revolution":"After defeating the Nationalists in the civil war, Mao proclaimed the People's Republic on 1 October 1949. Land reform, collectivisation and later campaigns transformed China.",
"Decolonisation of Africa and Asia":"After 1945 nationalist movements and the weakness of European powers led to independence for India (1947), Indonesia (1949), Ghana (1957) and most of Africa by the 1960s. Some transitions were violent, as in Algeria and Kenya.",
"Bangladesh Liberation War":"In 1971 West Pakistan's army cracked down on East Pakistan after the Awami League won elections; millions fled to India. India intervened in December, and Pakistan's forces surrendered in Dhaka on 16 December.",
"Moon landing":"On 20 July 1969 Apollo 11's Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed on the Moon, the climax of the US–Soviet space race.",
"Fall of the Berlin Wall":"On 9 November 1989 East Germans crossed the Wall after a confused announcement; Germany reunified in 1990.",
"Dissolution of the Soviet Union":"After Gorbachev's reforms and independence movements, the USSR dissolved in December 1991 into 15 states.",
"Indian economic liberalisation":"A 1991 balance-of-payments crisis led Narasimha Rao and Manmohan Singh to dismantle the licence raj, open trade and welcome foreign investment.",
"September 11 attacks":"Al-Qaeda hijackers flew planes into the World Trade Center and Pentagon, killing nearly 3,000; the US invaded Afghanistan and Iraq.",
"2004 Indian Ocean tsunami":"A magnitude 9.1 earthquake off Sumatra on 26 December 2004 caused tsunamis that killed about 230,000 people in 14 countries, including over 10,000 in India.",
"Chandrayaan-3 lands on the Moon":"On 23 August 2023 ISRO's Vikram lander touched down near the lunar south pole, making India the first country to land there.",
}
